{"id":"cluster-administration-logging","corpus":"k8s","title":"Logging Architecture","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/logging/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Logging Architecture"},{"kind":"p","text":"Application logs can help you understand what is happening inside your application. The logs are particularly useful for debugging problems and monitoring cluster activity. Most modern applications have some kind of logging mechanism. Likewise, container engines are designed to support logging. The easiest and most adopted logging method for containerized applications is writing to standard output and standard error streams."},{"kind":"p","text":"However, the native functionality provided by a container engine or runtime is usually not enough for a complete logging solution."},{"kind":"p","text":"For example, you may want to access your application's logs if a container crashes, a pod gets evicted, or a node dies."},{"kind":"p","text":"In a cluster, logs should have a separate storage and lifecycle independent of nodes, pods, or containers. This concept is called cluster-level logging."},{"kind":"p","text":"Cluster-level logging architectures require a separate backend to store, analyze, and query logs. Kubernetes does not provide a native storage solution for log data. Instead, there are many logging solutions that integrate with Kubernetes. The following sections describe how to handle and store logs on nodes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pod and container logs"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes captures logs from each container in a running Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"This example uses a manifest for a Pod with a container that writes text to the standard output stream, once per second."},{"kind":"p","text":"To run this pod, use the following command:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output is:"},{"kind":"p","text":"To fetch the logs, use the kubectl logs command, as follows:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output is similar to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use kubectl logs --previous to retrieve logs from a previous instantiation of a container. If your pod has multiple containers, specify which container's logs you want to access by appending a container name to the command, with a -c flag, like so:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Container log streams"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the PodLogsQuerySplitStreams feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"As an alpha feature, the kubelet can split out the logs from the two standard streams produced by a container: standard output and standard error. To use this behavior, you must enable the PodLogsQuerySplitStreams feature gate. With that feature gate enabled, Kubernetes 1.37 allows access to these log streams directly via the Pod API. You can fetch a specific stream by specifying the stream name (either Stdout or Stderr), using the stream query string. You must have access to read the log subresource of that Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"To demonstrate this feature, you can create a Pod that periodically writes text to both the standard output and error stream."},{"kind":"p","text":"To run this pod, use the following command:"},{"kind":"p","text":"To fetch only the stderr log stream, you can run:"},{"kind":"p","text":"See the kubectl logs documentation for more details."},{"kind":"h3","text":"How nodes handle container logs"},{"kind":"p","text":"A container runtime handles and redirects any output generated to a containerized application's stdout and stderr streams. Different container runtimes implement this in different ways; however, the integration with the kubelet is standardized as the CRI logging format."},{"kind":"p","text":"By default, if a container restarts, the kubelet keeps one terminated container with its logs. If a pod is evicted from the node, all corresponding containers are also evicted, along with their logs."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet makes logs available to clients via a special feature of the Kubernetes API. The usual way to access this is by running kubectl logs."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Log rotation"},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet is responsible for rotating container logs and managing the logging directory structure. The kubelet sends this information to the container runtime (using CRI), and the runtime writes the container logs to the given location."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can configure two kubelet configuration settings, containerLogMaxSize (default 10Mi) and containerLogMaxFiles (default 5), using the kubelet configuration file. These settings let you configure the maximum size for each log file and the maximum number of files allowed for each container respectively."},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to perform an efficient log rotation in clusters where the volume of the logs generated by the workload is large, kubelet also provides a mechanism to tune how the logs are rotated in terms of how many concurrent log rotations can be performed and the interval at which the logs are monitored and rotated as required. You can configure two kubelet configuration settings, containerLogMaxWorkers and containerLogMonitorInterval using the kubelet configuration file."}],"lemmas":{"logging":["logging","log"],"loggings":["logging","loggings"],"logged":["logged","log"],"logs":["log","logs"],"helps":["help","helps"],"helping":["helping","help"],"helped":["helped","help"],"understands":["understands","understand"],"understanding":["understanding","understand"],"understanded":["understanded","understand"],"understood":["understood","understand"],"happening":["happening","happen"],"happenings":["happening","happenings"],"happens":["happ